    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A federation of the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, with a common coinage stamped with the Boeotian shield.</li><li>Its fifth-century constitution of eleven districts and a representative council of 660 is one of the best documented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> it broke Spartan power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE and briefly led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> spent several years as a hostage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> in the 360s BCE, at the height of Theban power, and probably learned from its generals.</p></aside>
    </section>

