    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thucydides"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thucydides.jpg" alt="Thucydides" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thucydides_pushkin01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">user:shakko, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, the fertile plain of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>, held a dozen or more independent cities, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> was the largest, followed by Orchomenus, Thespiae, Tanagra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. The Boeotians shared an Aeolic dialect, a tradition of common descent and cults such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> Itonia near Coronea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onchestus">Onchestus</a>, which gave a basis for joint action. In the second half of the sixth century BCE Thebes began to draw its neighbours into a federation, and a common coinage stamped with the figure-of-eight Boeotian shield appeared around this time. Resistance was strong where Thebes was feared: Plataea, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, placed itself under Athenian protection, traditionally in 519 BCE, and in 506 BCE the Boeotians and Chalcidians were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. During the Persian invasion of 480–479 BCE Thebes and most Boeotian cities took the Persian side, and Boeotians fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> at the Battle of Plataea in 479 BCE. Afterwards the victorious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> besieged Thebes and executed its pro-Persian leaders, and the federation lost its cohesion. Between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oenophyta">Battle of Oenophyta</a> in 457 BCE and 447 BCE Athens dominated Boeotia, until Boeotian exiles defeated an Athenian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-coronea-447-bc">Battle of Coronea</a> in 447 BCE. The federal state was then refounded on a more formal footing.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/athens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="Athens" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Parlette, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> was one of the best farming regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, with wheat and barley lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-copais">Lake Copais</a> and the Theban and Tanagran plains, good horse pasture and the eels of Lake Copais, a delicacy prized at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Its wealth rested on landowners, and the federal constitution of the late fifth century reserved full rights in the member cities to citizens who met a property qualification, generally understood as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Athenian writers mocked the Boeotians as heavy, rustic and fond of food, a stereotype that the region’s cultural record contradicts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar of Thebes</a> was the greatest choral lyric poet of the fifth century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinna">Corinna of Tanagra</a> was remembered as his rival, and the much earlier poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> had lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascra">Ascra</a> below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-helicon">Mount Helicon</a>. The sanctuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a> on Helicon, the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trophonius">Trophonius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livadeia">Lebadea</a> and the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptoion">Apollo Ptoios</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acraephia">Acraephia</a> drew visitors from across Greece. Under Theban leadership in the fourth century Pythagorean teaching reached the elite through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysis-of-taras">Lysis of Tarentum</a>, who taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Tanagra gave its name to the painted terracotta figurines found in its graves. Trade was modest compared with Corinth or Athens, as Boeotia had few good harbours, but the federal coinage circulated widely in central Greece.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-delium"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-delium.jpg" alt="Battle of Delium" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-delium">Battle of Delium</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_at_the_Battle_of_Delium_(Thomas_Couture,_1843).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Couture, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Boeotian strength lay in its heavy infantry and cavalry. Under the fifth-century constitution each of the eleven districts supplied about 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> and 100 horsemen, giving a federal army of some 11,000 hoplites and 1,100 cavalry. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-delium">Battle of Delium</a> in 424 BCE the Theban commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagondas">Pagondas</a> drew up the Thebans 25 ranks deep and used cavalry to break the Athenian line, an early sign of the tactics that later made Thebes famous. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> Boeotian forces killed the Spartan commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-haliartus">Battle of Haliartus</a> in 395 BCE and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-coronea-394-bc">Battle of Coronea</a> in 394 BCE. After 378 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> formed the Sacred Band, an elite unit of 300 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> used it to defeat a larger Spartan force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tegyra">Battle of Tegyra</a> in 375 BCE. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Battle of Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> massed his left wing 50 ranks deep against the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus I</a>, who was killed, shattering the reputation of Spartan arms. Epaminondas then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>, built a fleet in 364 BCE and died winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-362-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a> in 362 BCE. At the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Chaeronea</a> in 338 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> defeated Thebes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the Sacred Band was destroyed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hellenica-oxyrhynchia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hellenica-oxyrhynchia.jpg" alt="Hellenica Oxyrhynchia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hellenica-oxyrhynchia">Hellenica Oxyrhynchia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HellenicaOxyrhynchia_Walker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Издательство Кларендон, Оксфорд, 1913 г., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution of the refounded league is described by the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenica-oxyrhynchia">Hellenica Oxyrhynchia</a></em>, a historical fragment found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> that covers the years around 395 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> was divided into eleven districts, each of which elected one boeotarch, sent 60 members to a federal council of 660 and supplied troops, taxes and judges in proportion. Thebes controlled four districts, two of its own and two that it had taken over after destroying Plataea in 427 BCE, while Orchomenus and Thespiae each held two with their dependent towns, Tanagra one, and smaller cities shared the rest. Each member city was governed by four councils of propertied citizens that took turns to prepare business, and the federal council met on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>. After the King’s Peace dissolved the league in 386 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoebidas">Spartan Phoebidas</a> seized the Cadmea, the citadel of Thebes, in 382 BCE, Theban exiles led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> recovered the city in the winter of 379–378 BCE. The new state was more democratic and more Theban: an assembly of all Boeotian citizens meeting at Thebes elected seven boeotarchs, and smaller cities lost much of their independence, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, Thespiae and Orchomenus were destroyed for resisting. The Hellenistic league kept boeotarchs and a federal assembly under an annual federal archon.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thebes-greece.jpg" alt="Thebes, Greece" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes, Greece</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_(GR03)_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chabe01, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Boeotian League is among the best documented Greek federal states, or <em>koina</em>, and its representative council, proportional to the military and financial contributions of each district, is a rare example of representation in the ancient world. For a decade after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> the Theban-led league transformed Greek politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> freed Messenia from Spartan control and founded the city of Messene in 369 BCE, and the Thebans supported the new Arcadian federation and its capital at Megalopolis, permanently breaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>’s hold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> extended Theban influence into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a>, and the young Philip II spent several years as a hostage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> in the 360s BCE, where he probably observed Theban methods of war. In this way the Theban hegemony helped to prepare the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> that ended it. The tactics of Leuctra, with a deep, weighted wing and the coordination of infantry and cavalry, were studied by later commanders from Philip and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> to modern military theorists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> also gave Greek literature <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> and, under Roman rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch of Chaeronea</a>, whose lives of Pelopidas and Epaminondas, the latter now lost, shaped the later image of the Theban heroes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae-364-bce"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae-364-bce.jpg" alt="Battle of Cynoscephalae (364 BCE)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae-364-bce">Battle of Cynoscephalae (364 BCE)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:362BCThebanHegemony.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Megistias, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theban supremacy did not outlast its leaders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae-364-bce">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a> in 364 BCE fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-pherae">Alexander of Pherae</a>, and the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> at Mantinea in 362 BCE left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> without a commander of his stature. The Third Sacred War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> from 356 to 346 BCE drained Boeotian resources and drew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, who became the arbiter of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. After the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Chaeronea</a> in 338 BCE a Macedonian garrison occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a>, and when Thebes revolted in 335 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> destroyed the city and sold its population into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, with the approval of Boeotian cities that Thebes had oppressed. The league was reorganised without Thebes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Orchomenus were restored. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> rebuilt Thebes in 316 BCE, and it rejoined the league in the third century BCE, probably in the 280s. The Hellenistic league was a second-rank power that shifted between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> and the Achaean League, and was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> near Chaeronea around 245 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> described a society weakened by debt and public feasting. When Boeotian cities leaned towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a>, Roman envoys led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-marcius-philippus">Quintus Marcius Philippus</a> persuaded them to break away separately, and the league was dissolved in 171 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Boeotian League was one of the earliest and best documented federal states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, with a representative council and a strong citizen army, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> it broke Spartan power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE and briefly made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> the leading state in Greece.</em></p>

